{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"Split from silvern-dewy-damselfly (f4cf-60b2-41c0-4843) during its plan review.\n\n## Problem\n\nAfter a close whose completion-signature append failed, a later reader cannot reliably tell\nthat ticket apart from one closed with the gate off. Damselfly makes the loss machine-readable\nin the RETURNED PAYLOAD of the call that suffered it, but that payload is ephemeral — it is\nnever persisted (`_lib_writes.py:424`, `_commands/transition.py:591`,\n`schemas/transition_result.schema.json`). An agent that was not the caller, or the same agent\nin a later session, has no durable discriminator.\n\n## Premise verified in code — PARTIALLY true, narrower than first written\n\nThe original draft of this ticket claimed all three states are indistinguishable. That\noverstates it; verification found one existing discriminator:\n\n**The `COMPLETION_VERDICT` PASS sidecar** (`llm/completion_sidecar.py:202`\n`latest_pass_record`, tag `completion_verifier_pass_v1`) is emitted on EVERY completion PASS at\n`_commands/close_precheck.py:577-580`, before signing and before the close commits. So:\n\n| state | `attestations[\"completion-verifier\"]` | PASS sidecar |\n|---|---|---|\n| (a) closed, attestation valid | present | present |\n| (b) closed, signature failed to persist | absent | **present** |\n| (c) closed, gate OFF | absent | absent |\n\n(b) is therefore already distinguishable via `audit/read.py:113-123,203-210`. Three caveats\nkeep it from being a real answer: the sidecar is **reducer-ignored**\n(`completion_sidecar.py:10-16` — not in `KNOWN_EVENT_TYPES`, so it never reaches compiled state\nand `rebar show` cannot see it, only the audit surface); its emit is **best-effort**, dropped\nwith only a stderr warning (`close_precheck.py:349-355`); and `prune()` deletes it after\n`RETAIN_PER_TICKET = 10` (`completion_sidecar.py:44,123`).\n\n**(c) has no discriminator at all, and that is the sharper bug.** The gate's on/off state at\nclose time is nowhere in the store — it is read from config and discarded\n(`_commands/close_precheck.py:410-416`; key `verify.require_completion_verification_for_close`\nat `_config_schema.py:159`, default False). So \"closed with no attestation\" collapses these\ninto ONE indistinguishable state:\n\n* gate genuinely off (`close_precheck.py:416`)\n* `--force` close (`close_precheck.py:417`)\n* config unreadable → `gate_enabled` **fails OPEN** (`_commands/gates.py:79-87`)\n* `source == \"local\"` unattested close (`close_precheck.py:585-586`)\n* `certifiable is False` withholding (`close_precheck.py:589-600`)\n* a dropped PASS sidecar\n\nWorse, `txn.transition_core` **accepts `force_close_reason` and never uses it** — the parameter\nat `_commands/txn.py:198` occurs exactly once in the file and is silently discarded, though\n`transition_close.py:474` passes it. A force close leaves no durable reason on the STATUS\nevent. (Tracked separately; see the linked bug.)\n\nThe close STATUS event records only `status`, `current_status`, `parent_status_uuid`, and\npresent-only `close_class` (`txn.py:277-296`; documented `docs/event-schema.md:47`).\n\n## Direction\n\nRecord, on the close STATUS event, WHY a completion signature was or was not expected — in the\nsame locked write that already commits the close (`txn.transition_core`), so NO additional lock\nacquisition is needed. That is the whole point: an audit comment or a second event would append\nthrough the same `event_append` -> `write_lock` path whose 60s timeout causes the failure being\nrecorded, and would be missing exactly when it matters.\n\n**It must be an ENUM, not a boolean.** A single bool cannot separate the states the ACs demand.\nThe field is `completion_expectation`, total over the close path:\n\n| value | meaning at close time |\n|---|---|\n| `required` | the gate ran and a signature was expected |\n| `gate_off` | `verify.require_completion_verification_for_close` was false |\n| `force_bypassed` | `--force` skipped the gate by operator approval |\n| `local_source` | unattested local-source close (`close_precheck.py:585-586`) |\n| `not_certifiable` | `certifiable is False` withholding (`close_precheck.py:589-600`) |\n| `not_applicable` | `idea -> closed`, a REJECT/DROP rather than a completion |\n\n**The event records the EXPECTATION only, never the outcome.** The outcome is not knowable at\nthat point: the STATUS event commits BEFORE signing is attempted, which is the ordering that\ncauses this whole bug family. A reader derives the three states by combining the durable\nexpectation with the durable attestation:\n\n* `required` + attestation present -> closed and signed\n* `required` + attestation absent -> **closed with the signature LOST** (the state damselfly\n  can only report ephemerally)\n* anything else -> not expected, and the value says exactly why\n\n**How the value is computed and threaded.** It is NOT `verified_result`. That is `None` for a\n`--force` close even though a signature WAS expected, and `None` for gate-off, so it conflates\nthe very states this ticket separates. `close_precheck` already distinguishes all six cases at\nthe points cited above but discards the distinction, returning only a verdict-or-`None`. So:\n`_completion_precheck` returns the expectation alongside its verdict, `close_ticket` passes it\ninto `txn.transition_core`, and `transition_core` — which today accepts no such parameter —\ngains one and writes it into the STATUS event `data` next to the present-only `close_class`\n(`txn.py:287-288`), following that field's present-only precedent.\n\nDamselfly's ephemeral `completion_signature.cause` stays as-is and is NOT the same vocabulary:\nit reports an OUTCOME to the caller (`signed` / `sign_failed` / `material_drifted` /\n`force_bypassed`), where this records an EXPECTATION durably. Where they overlap\n(`force_bypassed`) the spelling is deliberately shared.\n\nEvent-schema addition: STATUS field, reducer handling, and `docs/event-schema.md`, present-only\nso an older reader that does not know it degrades safely — the preserved-and-ignored precedent\n`COMPLETION_VERDICT` already follows.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] `completion_expectation` is written into the close STATUS event in the SAME locked write\n      as the close, taking no additional write-lock — verified by a test asserting the lock is\n      acquired exactly once\n- [ ] A later reader, holding no lock and reading only reduced state, separates\n      closed-and-signed (`required` + attestation) from closed-with-signature-lost (`required`,\n      no attestation) from closed-with-gate-off (`gate_off`) — verified by a test closing one\n      ticket each way and asserting all three read back distinctly\n- [ ] Each of `force_bypassed`, `local_source` and `not_certifiable` is recorded as ITSELF and\n      never as `gate_off` — verified by a test per value\n- [ ] `idea -> closed` records `not_applicable`, matching damselfly's rule that a REJECT/DROP\n      is not a completion — verified by a test\n- [ ] The expectation is computed independently of `verified_result`, so a `--force` close\n      still records `force_bypassed` rather than being conflated with gate-off — verified by a\n      test\n- [ ] The field is documented in `docs/event-schema.md` and is present-only, so a reader that\n      does not know it degrades safely — verified by a test"}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786563223633395001,"uuid":"7ce74d57-0eb0-4a94-a0eb-faca3f6e0d68"}